Calendar events that fire an un-ignorable alarm — active effort to silence, via the phone app + xMatters bridge.

Make calendar events fire an un-ignorable alarm — one that takes active effort to silence — to stop important appointments being missed. The original problem (Google/clock reminders are too easy to swipe away) still stands; what changed is the *delivery mechanism* — the alarms now go to xMatters, the house escalation channel that already demands acknowledgement, wired into the fleet via `super/bin/alert`. So the hard part is solved; calendaralarm becomes the thin bridge — calendar reader → imminence/importance decision → `alert --critical` — that decides which events are worth escalating. The reader exists (`~/calendaralarm/upcoming.py`, Google Calendar API); the missing middle is the "what's imminent and important enough to page me" logic and its scheduler.
